I am betting, however, that you are doing this, and am figuring it might be=
=20
the cause of our different results.  What do you get if you load the=20
patched "snd-es18xx" driver using only "isapnp=3D0"?

I'm not surprised the snd-sb8 driver worked for you.  From what I could tel=
l=20
from the drivers, the ESS chips are basically Sound Blasters with a couple =
of=20
(inconsistent and sometimes buggy) extensions.  The snd-sb8 driver also=20
worked okay for me.

=46rom the code, it seems that dual DMA is required for simultaneous playba=
ck=20
and recording (the so called full-duplex).  The snd-sb8 driver only support=
s=20
one DMA, so it does not do any work to figure out where the interrupt came=
=20
from: it checks the driver's software state (playback or capture).

The code for the "snd-es18xx" seems to indicate the second DMA is somewhat=
=20
broken anyway, so you probably don't have anything to loose by going with=20
either the patched "snd-es18xx" DMA or the "snd-sb8".  To quote the source:

