On Fri June 9 2006 10:01, Michael Galloway wrote:
> what is the correct method to upgrade kernels via apt? i've install the s=
mp
> kernel with apt and now in /boot there is:

Aboot looks for the file "[partition]/etc/aboot.conf" (where parition is=20
hardcoded into it and can be viewed and set via the "abootconf" program) to=
=20
tell it what kernel and initrd image to load.  I'm guessing you /boot is on=
 a=20
seperate parition, so the file to have a look at is "/boot/etc/aboot.conf"=
=20
(if it's the same as your root, then the file would be "/etc/aboot.conf").

It will have a bunch of lines to the effect of:

[flag]:[partition]/<kernel image> initrd=3D/<initrd image>
=2E..

were flag and parition are numbers.  Your run a command in SRM such as "boo=
t=20
dkc0 -fl 0" (you can automate this by setting appropriate SRM variables)=20
which loads aboot off of dkc0 and passes it the flag 0.  Then aboot loads t=
he=20
kernel and initrd image that match that flag 0 in the "aboot.conf" file.

The only thing to really be aware of is that the paths are all relative to =
the=20
root of the parition specified in the "aboot.conf" file.  So if /boot is on=
=20
its own partition, and that parition is the first parition, you should have=
=20
lines like:

0:1/vmlinuz initrd=3D/initrd.img
1:1/vmlinuz-2.6.15-1-alpha-smp initrd=3D/initrd.img-2.6.15-1-alpha-smp
2:1/vmlinuz-2.6.15-1-alpha-generic initrd=3D/initrd.img-2.6.15-1-alpha-gene=
ric

The first one boots the latest kernel you have installed (installing a kern=
el=20
changes the vmlinuz and initrd.img links to point to it).

The second and third are probably not there, but I would put them in just s=
o I=20
have additional boot options (in case something goes wrong).  Usually there=
=20
are maintained vmlinuz.old and initrd.img.old links and appropriate entries=
=20
as for this purpose (but I don't see them in your directory listing).

Truth be told, you don't need any of them other than for convience.  If abo=
ot=20
gets passed a non-existant flag, it gives you a prompt, and you can manual=
=20
specify everything via the command "b [parition]/<kernel> initrd=3D<initrd=
=20
image>" (there are other commands for listing files, and so on).

Later!  -T

=2D-=20
