I was fooling around on my Alpha and just noticed that all the special IEEE=
=20
numbers (infs, nans, and denormals) are now being replaced by zeros.

=46or example,

  #include <stdio.h>

  int main() {
    double value1;
    double value2;

    printf("Please enter the num: ");
    scanf("%lf",&value1);
    printf("Please enter the den: ");
    scanf("%lf",&value2);
    printf("\n%g/%g =3D %g\n",value1,value2,value1/value2);

    return 0;
  }

saved to "div.c" and compiled with "gcc -o div -lm -Wall div.c" gives

  0/0 =3D 0 (should be nan)
  1/0 =3D 0 (should be inf)
  1e-320/2 =3D 0 (should be 5e-321: 1e-320 is a denormalized)

Dumping the generated code with "objdump -d div | less" shows gcc is=20
generating all the required trap instructions and su extentions on the=20
floating point operations.  This leaves me wondering if something has chang=
ed=20
in the IEEE floating point fixup code in newer kernels.

Unfortunately, I'm stuck at 2.6.15 or greater due to new udev requirements=
=20
(and only 2.6.16 is available), but is there anyone else out there running =
an=20
older kernel (maybe even 2.4) that could give this a try?

Thanks!  -Tyson

